<div class="container">

<header class="main-header">
  <div class="book-title">Likutay Halachos &middot; Orach Chaim</div>
  <h1>Hilchos Rosh HaShanah</h1>
  <div class="subtitle">Halacha 3</div>
  <div class="halacha-num">3 Oaseyos</div>
  <div class="source-ref-header">The matter of Rosh HaShanah and Yom Kippur and the blowing of the Shofar on Rosh HaShanah.<br>Based on the ma&rsquo;amar &ldquo;Markevos Par&rsquo;oh V&rsquo;Chailo&rdquo;<br>[&ldquo;The chariots of Pharaoh and his army&rdquo;]<br>(siman 38). See there well.</div>
</header>

<!-- INTRO -->
<div class="intro">
<p class="no-indent">For one needs to elevate the <span class="concept"><span class="heb-term">dibbur</span></span> [speech] and rectify it, for from the <span class="concept"><span class="heb-term">p&rsquo;gam ha&rsquo;dibbur</span></span> [damage to speech] a <span class="concept"><span class="heb-term">ru&rsquo;ach s&rsquo;arah</span></span> [stormy wind] is made, etc. (see there). And this ru&rsquo;ach s&rsquo;arah is a great <span class="heb-term">m&rsquo;katreig</span> [accuser] from which come all the <span class="heb-term">kitrugin</span> [accusations] and <span class="heb-term">nisyonos</span> [trials], and it is &ldquo;after the matters,&rdquo; etc. And one needs to subjugate the <span class="concept"><span class="heb-term">Sitra D&rsquo;Kaitz Kol Basar</span></span> [the side of &ldquo;the end of all flesh&rdquo;], which is the ru&rsquo;ach s&rsquo;arah mentioned above, beneath the <span class="heb-term">dibbur di&rsquo;k&rsquo;dushah</span> [speech of holiness], etc. And one needs to elevate the dibbur to its <span class="heb-term">shoresh</span> [root], which is the <span class="heb-term">z&rsquo;roa</span> [arm] &mdash; which is the <span class="concept">five fingers of the left hand</span>, which are the <span class="concept">five <span class="heb-term">g&rsquo;vuros</span></span> [severities], which are the aspect of the <span class="concept">five <span class="heb-term">motzaos ha&rsquo;peh</span></span> [points of articulation of the mouth]. And the beginning of the ascent of the dibbur is from its head &mdash; namely, from <span class="concept">the choicest emes</span> [truth] that exists in the dibbur, etc.</p>

<p>And the tikkun of the dibbur is through the <span class="concept">Torah that one learns when in distress</span> &mdash; in poverty and hardship &mdash; which is the aspect of <span class="heb-term">lailah</span> [night], for then is the dominion of <span class="heb-term">Kaitz Kol Basar</span>. And our Sages, of blessed memory, said: &ldquo;One who learns Torah at night &mdash; a thread of <span class="heb-term">chesed</span> [kindness] is drawn upon him by day.&rdquo; And chesed is the aspect of <span class="concept"><span class="heb-term">boker d&rsquo;Avraham</span></span> [the morning of Avraham]. And through this, all the accusers that rule over the dibbur fall. And then the dibbur goes out in <span class="heb-term">shir</span> [song] and <span class="heb-term">shevach</span> [praise] and <span class="heb-term">hallel</span> [acclaim] to HaKadosh Baruch Hu, etc.</p>

<p>And when one elevates the dibbur to its root &mdash; namely, to the g&rsquo;vuros &mdash; and begins to <span class="concept">speak between oneself and one&rsquo;s Maker</span> in the flame of the g&rsquo;vuros, and arouses oneself to Hashem, blessed be He, then the <span class="heb-term">or shoresh</span> [light of the root] enters, etc. And one begins to speak with the <span class="concept">warmth of the heart</span> &mdash; words of emes that are in the heart, etc. &mdash; in an awakening of t&rsquo;shuvah [repentance], and one sees one&rsquo;s lowliness and the greatness of the Creator. And then a <span class="concept">great <span class="heb-term">bushah</span></span> [shame] enters him over his transgressions, as before a great ruler, etc. And this bushah is not yet in actuality [<span class="heb-term">b&rsquo;po&rsquo;al</span>], etc. But afterward, when one does t&rsquo;shuvah and removes the <span class="heb-term">tipshus</span> [foolishness] from oneself, and one&rsquo;s <span class="heb-term">seichel</span> [intellect] is increased, then the <span class="concept">bushah is revealed upon one&rsquo;s face</span>, and this bushah is the aspect of the <span class="concept">light of the <span class="heb-term">T&rsquo;fillin</span></span>, etc. And this is the meaning of: <span class="verse">&ldquo;Then you will understand the fear of Hashem&rdquo;</span> <span class="verse-source">(Mishlai 2:5)</span>, etc., and <span class="verse">&ldquo;All the peoples of the earth will see that the Name of Hashem is called upon you, and they will fear you,&rdquo;</span> etc. <span class="verse">&ldquo;And the knowledge of G-d you will find,&rdquo;</span> etc.</p>
</div>

<!-- OAS 1 -->
<div class="section-marker">1</div>

<p class="no-indent">And this is the aspect of <span class="concept">Rosh HaShanah and the blowing of the Shofar</span>. For on Rosh HaShanah, the great m&rsquo;katreig goes out &mdash; which is the aspect of the ru&rsquo;ach s&rsquo;arah mentioned above, the aspect of Kaitz Kol Basar. And one needs to subjugate it beneath the dibbur of holiness, and one needs to elevate and rectify the dibbur. For its main sustenance [<span class="heb-term">y&rsquo;nikah</span>] is from the dibbur, which is the aspect of <span class="heb-term">Malchus</span> [Kingship]. For all our <span class="heb-term">avodah</span> [service] on Rosh HaShanah is for the sake of revealing His Kingship, blessed be He, which is through the tikkun of the dibbur, which is the aspect of Malchus. For the dibbur, which is the aspect of Malchus, was damaged through the sin of Adam HaRishon [the first man], who damaged through <span class="heb-term">azus</span> [brazenness], which is the p&rsquo;gam of the dibbur (as is explained in the aforementioned ma&rsquo;amar further on; see there well). And we need on Rosh HaShanah and Yom Kippur to rectify the sin of Adam HaRishon (as is brought in the K&rsquo;savim [writings of the Arizal]).</p>

<p>And therefore we need to elevate the dibbur to its root and rectify it, in order to reveal His Kingship, blessed be He, through the rectified dibbur that goes out in song and praise and acclaim to HaKadosh Baruch Hu. And to merit through the dibbur the aspect of <span class="concept">bushah</span>, which is the aspect of <span class="concept"><span class="heb-term">yir&rsquo;ah</span></span> [awe/fear] that is drawn through recognizing the Creator, blessed be He, through the t&rsquo;shuvah that one merits through the dibbur (as is explained and understood, all this, in the aforementioned ma&rsquo;amar).</p>

<p>And the main elevation of the dibbur is through <span class="concept">the Torah that one learns when in distress</span>, in poverty and hardship. And through this one merits <span class="concept">chesed</span>, the aspect of boker d&rsquo;Avraham. And this is the aspect of the <span class="concept">kol Shofar</span> [sound of the Shofar]. For the Shofar is the aspect of <span class="heb-term">Mattan Torah</span> [the Giving of the Torah], which was given with the sound of the Shofar. And through the kol Shofar we invoke the merit of the Torah. And through this we draw the chesed of Avraham. For the kol Shofar is the aspect of the Torah that we learn in distress and hardship, in the aspect of <span class="verse">&ldquo;From the narrow place I called to Y-ah&rdquo;</span> <span class="verse-source">(T&rsquo;hillim 118)</span> &mdash; which is the aspect of the <span class="concept">narrow place of the Shofar</span>. And this is the aspect of Mattan Torah, which was with the kol Shofar. For the Torah was given in the <span class="heb-term">midbar</span> [wilderness], in an arid land &mdash; which hints that the main Torah is when one upholds it from poverty and hardship, which is the aspect of a wilderness, an arid land. And therefore the Shofar invokes the merit of the Torah that was given in the wilderness, which is the aspect of the Torah that one learns in distress and hardship, which is the aspect of the narrow place of the Shofar, as above.</p>

<p>And through this, <span class="concept">chesed of Avraham</span> is drawn to subdue the Sitra D&rsquo;Kaitz Kol Basar &mdash; to subdue all the accusers. For all the accusers are subdued through the kol of the Shofar, as is known &mdash; namely, through the chesed of Avraham that is aroused through the kol Shofar, which is the aspect of the merit of the Torah that one learns in distress and hardship, as above. And then, when one elevates the dibbur and begins to speak between oneself and one&rsquo;s Maker in the flame of the g&rsquo;vuros, etc., words of emes that are in the heart, etc.</p>

<!-- OAS 2 -->
<div class="section-marker">2</div>

<p class="no-indent">And this is the aspect of <span class="concept">T&rsquo;kiah, Sh&rsquo;varim, T&rsquo;ruah</span>. <span class="concept">T&rsquo;kiah</span> &mdash; this is the aspect of chesed of Avraham that is drawn through the kol of the Shofar, as above. <span class="concept">Sh&rsquo;varim</span> &mdash; this is the aspect of <span class="heb-term">g&rsquo;vuros</span> [severities], the aspect of a <span class="heb-term">laiv nishbar</span> [broken heart] &mdash; namely, that one speaks between oneself and one&rsquo;s Maker in the flame of the g&rsquo;vuros and breaks the heart in an awakening of t&rsquo;shuvah. And this is the aspect of Sh&rsquo;varim. <span class="concept">T&rsquo;ruah</span> &mdash; this is the aspect of Yaakov, the aspect of emes, as is brought. For the main elevation of the dibbur is from the choicest emes, etc., as above. And this is the aspect of the T&rsquo;ruah, which is the aspect of emes, as above.</p>

<p>And through all of this, <span class="concept">bushah</span> [shame] is drawn &mdash; which is the inclusiveness of all colors [<span class="heb-term">k&rsquo;laliyus ha&rsquo;g&rsquo;vanin</span>]. And this is the aspect of the <span class="concept">reddening of the face of the one who blows</span> [<span class="heb-term">his&rsquo;admus p&rsquo;nai ha&rsquo;tokei&rsquo;a</span>] &mdash; this is the aspect of the inclusiveness of all colors, the aspect of bushah, where <span class="heb-term">azal chivra v&rsquo;asei sumka</span> [the whiteness departs and redness comes]. For the main tikkun is through the bushah (as is explained there in the aforementioned ma&rsquo;amar). And this is the aspect of the <span class="concept">yir&rsquo;ah</span> [awe] that is drawn through the kol Shofar, as is written: <span class="verse">&ldquo;Is a Shofar blown in a city and the people do not tremble?&rdquo;</span> <span class="verse-source">(Amos 3:6)</span>. For the yir&rsquo;ah is the bushah, as is explained there.</p>

<p>And the yir&rsquo;ah that is drawn upon all the world through the one who blows the Shofar &mdash; this is the aspect of <span class="verse">&ldquo;All the peoples of the earth will see that the Name of Hashem is called upon you, and they will fear you.&rdquo;</span> For the reddening of the face of the one who blows is the aspect of the bushah that is revealed upon his face, and through this, bushah and yir&rsquo;ah are drawn upon all who gaze upon him, in the aspect of &ldquo;and they will see &hellip; and they will fear you&rdquo; (as is explained there; see there).</p>

<p>And the <span class="concept">final T&rsquo;kiah</span> is to draw the <span class="heb-term">mochin</span> [intellect/mentalities] into the <span class="heb-term">panim</span> [face/front]. For at first the bushah is not yet in actuality, but through the t&rsquo;shuvah, the yir&rsquo;ah and bushah are revealed upon one&rsquo;s face, through the additional recognition [<span class="heb-term">hakarah</span>] of the greatness of the Creator, blessed be He, that is gained through the t&rsquo;shuvah, as above. And this is the aspect of the final T&rsquo;kiah. For through the Sh&rsquo;varim-T&rsquo;ruah, which are the aspect of t&rsquo;shuvah, as above, through this one merits additional understanding [<span class="heb-term">havanah y&rsquo;sairah</span>] of His greatness, blessed be He.</p>

<p>And therefore all the world is aroused in t&rsquo;shuvah at the time of the blowing of the Shofar, for the main purpose of the Shofar is for this, as above. And through this, additional understanding is drawn, and then the bushah is revealed upon one&rsquo;s face. And this is the aspect of the final T&rsquo;kiah, the aspect of <span class="verse">&ldquo;Is a Shofar blown in a city and the people do not tremble?&rdquo;</span> &mdash; namely, the yir&rsquo;ah and bushah that is revealed upon one&rsquo;s face through the additional understanding that is gained through the t&rsquo;shuvah, which is the aspect of Sh&rsquo;varim-T&rsquo;ruah, as above. For the <span class="concept">Shofar is the aspect of T&rsquo;fillin</span>, the aspect of <span class="heb-term">Aim L&rsquo;Vinah</span> [&ldquo;Mother of Understanding&rdquo;]. For the Shofar is the aspect of <span class="heb-term">Binah</span> [Understanding], <span class="heb-term">Alma D&rsquo;Asai</span> [the World to Come], as is known. And this is the aspect of <span class="verse">&ldquo;Then you will understand the fear of Hashem&rdquo;</span> (as is explained there; see there well).</p>

<!-- OAS 3 -->
<div class="section-marker">3</div>

<p class="no-indent">And this is the aspect of what is found in the <span class="heb-term">Kavanos</span> [the mystical intentions of the Arizal]: that the <span class="concept">final T&rsquo;kiah is to draw the mochin into the panim</span>. For through the Sh&rsquo;varim-T&rsquo;ruah, which are the aspect of t&rsquo;shuvah, as above, through this, additional <span class="heb-term">da&rsquo;as</span> [knowledge] and recognition of His greatness, blessed be He, is gained, as above. And this is the aspect of the final T&rsquo;kiah &mdash; that the mochin enter &mdash; namely, that additional da&rsquo;as is gained, as above &mdash; which one merits through the awakening of t&rsquo;shuvah that occurs at the time of the blowing of the Shofar, which is the aspect of Sh&rsquo;varim-T&rsquo;ruah, as above. And through this one merits additional understanding, as above. And this is the aspect of the final T&rsquo;kiah &mdash; the aspect of the entry of the mochin into the panim, as above. And through this, bushah and yir&rsquo;ah are drawn. And this is the aspect of <span class="verse">&ldquo;Is a Shofar blown in a city and the people do not tremble?&rdquo;</span> etc. &mdash; the aspect of the final T&rsquo;kiah, as above.</p>

<p class="blessed">Blessed is Hashem forever, Amain and Amain.</p>

</div>

